1. Aim

To perform Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) on customer_data.csv by visualizing numerical data using histograms, selecting numerical columns, computing a correlation matrix and plotting a correlation heatmap.


CODE -

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

file_path = r""

data = pd.read_csv(file_path)

print(data.head())

data.hist(figsize=(10, 8), bins=20)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

sns.histplot(data["Age"], kde=True, bins=20)
plt.title("Age Distribution with KDE")
plt.xlabel("Age")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")
plt.show()

print(data.columns)

numeric_data = data.select_dtypes(include="number")
print(numeric_data.head())

corr = numeric_data.corr()
print(corr)

plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))
sns.heatmap(corr, annot=True, cmap="coolwarm", linewidths=0.5)
plt.title("Correlation Matrix Heatmap")
plt.show()





2. Theory

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA): EDA is the process of analyzing and visualizing a dataset to understand its characteristics, distributions and relationships between variables.

Histogram: A histogram represents the frequency distribution of numerical data using bars. It helps identify the distribution, spread and skewness of data.

Kernel Density Estimation (KDE): KDE estimates the probability density of a continuous variable and represents its distribution using a smooth curve.

Correlation Matrix: A correlation matrix shows the correlation coefficients between pairs of numerical variables. The Pearson correlation coefficient ranges from −1 to +1.

+1: Perfect positive linear correlation.
0: No linear correlation.
−1: Perfect negative linear correlation.

Heatmap: A heatmap represents numerical values using colours. It helps visualize the strength and direction of correlations between variables.

Libraries used:

Pandas — data loading and manipulation.
Matplotlib — plotting graphs.
Seaborn — statistical visualization.

